//? Title: Baby-Step Giant-Step: Discrete Logarithms by Meeting in the Middle
//? Subtitle: Shanks's square-root algorithm, the table size that minimises its work, and how fast it breaks small Diffie–Hellman keys, with datamunge's algorithms::baby_step_giant_step_trace
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a prime \(p\), a base \(g\) and a target \(h\), the *discrete logarithm* problem asks for
\(x\) with \(g^x\equiv h\pmod p\). Trying every exponent takes up to \(p-1\) multiplications.
Shanks's baby-step giant-step algorithm splits \(x=im+j\), tabulates the \(m\) "baby steps"
\(g^j\), and walks "giant steps" \(h\,g^{-mi}\) until one lands in the table. We derive its cost,
\(m+\lceil n/m\rceil\) in the worst case and \(m+n/(2m)\) on average for a group of order \(n\),
so the best table holds \(\sqrt n\) entries for the worst case but only \(\sqrt{n/2}\) for the
average. A sweep over table sizes at \(p=1{,}000{,}003\) matches both formulas. Across primes from
\(10^3\) to \(10^{12}\) the measured work grows as \(p^{0.500}\), and a 40-bit Diffie–Hellman
secret falls in about a tenth of a second on this machine. Finally, when \(g\) generates only a subgroup of
prime order \(q\), searching that subgroup costs \(\sqrt q\) instead of \(\sqrt p\), which is the
idea behind the Pohlig–Hellman algorithm.
)

\toc

> Meeting in the middle

Let \(g\) have order \(n\) modulo \(p\), so \(g^n\equiv1\) and the powers \(g^0,\dots,g^{n-1}\) are
distinct. When \(g\) is a primitive root, \(n=p-1\). Every \(x\in[0,n)\) can be written by the
division algorithm as

$$
x=i\,m+j,\qquad 0\le j<m,\qquad 0\le i<\lceil n/m\rceil .
$$
\alttext(x equals i times m plus j, with j from 0 to m minus 1 and i from 0 to the ceiling of n over m, minus 1.)

Multiplying \(g^{im+j}\equiv h\) by \(g^{-im}\) separates the two unknowns:

$$
g^{\,j}\;\equiv\;h\cdot\bigl(g^{-m}\bigr)^{i}\pmod p .
$$
\alttext(g to the j is congruent to h times g to the minus m, all to the i, modulo p.)

The left side depends only on \(j\), the right only on \(i\). The algorithm stores the \(m\)
*baby steps* \(g^j\) in a hash table, then computes the *giant steps* \(h,\ hg^{-m},\ hg^{-2m},\dots\),
one multiplication each, and looks each up. A hit at giant step \(i\) and table entry \(j\) gives
\(x=im+j\). Because the table keeps the smallest \(j\) for each value and \(i\) increases, the first
hit is the smallest solution. If \(h\) is not a power of \(g\), no giant step can hit, and after
\(\lceil n/m\rceil\) steps the algorithm reports that no logarithm exists.

```
BABY-STEP-GIANT-STEP(g, h, p, n, m):
    table ← {}
    e ← 1
    for j = 0 .. m−1:  table[e] ← j (keep the first);  e ← e·g mod p      # baby steps
    stride ← g^(−m) mod p                                                 # = g^(p−1−m)
    γ ← h
    for i = 0 .. ⌈n/m⌉−1:                                                 # giant steps
        if γ ∈ table: return i·m + table[γ]
        γ ← γ · stride mod p
    return NONE
```

`dm::algorithms::baby_step_giant_step_trace()` runs exactly this, with the group order and the
table size as optional arguments, and records the steps it took. The first block solves
\(2^x\equiv17\pmod{101}\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/baby_step_giant_step_collision.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::uint64_t g = 2, h = 17, p = 101;
  const auto t = alg::baby_step_giant_step_trace(g, h, p, 0, 0, true);
  std::print("Order of 2 mod 101: {}; table size m = {}.\n\n", alg::element_order(g, p), t.table_size);

  std::print("| step | baby \\(2^j\\) | giant \\(17\\cdot2^{{-10i}}\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.baby.size(); ++k)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", k, t.baby[k], k < t.giant.size() ? std::to_string(t.giant[k]) : "");
  std::print("\nMatch: giant step i = {} equals baby step j = {}, so x = {}·{} + {} = {}; check 2^{} mod 101 = {}.\n",
             t.giant_index, t.baby_index, t.giant_index, t.table_size, t.baby_index, t.exponent, t.exponent,
             alg::mod_pow(g, t.exponent, p));
  std::print("Work: {} baby steps + {} giant steps = {}, against {} for trying exponents in order.\n",
             t.baby_steps, t.giant_steps, t.baby_steps + t.giant_steps, t.exponent + 1);

  std::vector<double> bx, by, gx, gy;
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.baby.size(); ++k) {
    bx.push_back(static_cast<double>(t.baby[k]));
    by.push_back(static_cast<double>(k));
  }
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.giant.size(); ++k) {
    gx.push_back(static_cast<double>(t.giant[k]));
    gy.push_back(static_cast<double>(k));
  }
  const double hit = static_cast<double>(t.giant.back());
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .segments({hit}, {static_cast<double>(t.baby_index)}, {hit}, {static_cast<double>(t.giant_index)},
                dm::RGB{202, 138, 4}, 3.0)
      .lines(bx, by, "", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}, 1.0)
      .points(bx, by, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 9.0)
      .lines(gx, gy, "", dm::RGB{252, 165, 165}, 1.0)
      .points(gx, gy, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 9.0)
      .legend({"baby steps (j)", "giant steps (i)", "match"},
              {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{202, 138, 4}})
      .x_limits(0, 101)
      .y_limits(-0.5, 12)
      .title("Baby-step giant-step for 2^x = 17 (mod 101)")
      .x_label("residue mod 101")
      .y_label("step index (j or i)")
      .size(900, 500)
      .save_svg("build/baby_step_giant_step_collision.svg");
}
```
