\image(figures/baby_step_giant_step_scaling.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Mean time to recover a secret exponent against the size of the prime, on log scales: baby-step giant-step (blue) and trying exponents in order (red, up to \(10^8\)).)
\alttext(Line chart of log10 mean milliseconds against log10 p. The red brute-force line rises with slope about 1 from minus 3 at p equals 1000 to plus 2.3 at 10 to the 8. The blue baby-step giant-step line rises with slope about one half, from minus 3 to plus 2 at 10 to the 12.)

The number of steps is \(1.43\sqrt p\) to \(1.57\sqrt p\) at every size, close to the
\(1.5\sqrt p\) expected at \(m=\sqrt n\), and its log-log slope is 0.500. Time grows a little
faster (slope 0.56 from \(10^6\)) because the hash table outgrows the processor caches: at
\(10^{12}\) it holds a million entries, and the time per step rises from about 20 ns to 65 ns.
Trying exponents in order has slope 0.99, as expected for \(O(p)\), and is already 600 times
slower at \(10^8\).

A 40-bit prime (about \(10^{12}\)) gives no security: its secret falls in about 0.1 s. Each
extra factor of 100 in \(p\) costs 10 times more time and memory, so a 2048-bit prime would need
about \(2^{1024}\) steps. Real attacks on such primes use the index calculus instead, which is why
recommended Diffie–Hellman primes are so much larger than \(\sqrt p\) alone would suggest.

> Searching a subgroup

The order of any \(g\) divides \(p-1\) (Lagrange's theorem). If \(g\) has prime order \(q\), then
\(x\) only matters modulo \(q\), and the search can use \(n=q\): \(\sqrt q\) baby steps and at most
\(\sqrt q\) giant steps, however large \(p\) is. Pohlig and Hellman turned this into an attack on
any \(p\) for which \(p-1\) has only small prime factors: solve the logarithm in each prime-order
subgroup and combine the answers with the Chinese remainder theorem. That is why Diffie–Hellman
uses a *safe prime* \(p=2q+1\) with \(q\) prime, or a large prime-order subgroup.

The block builds a 12-digit prime \(p=kq+1\) around the prime \(q=1{,}000{,}003\), takes
\(g_q=g^{(p-1)/q}\), which has order \(q\), and compares searching with \(n=p-1\) and with \(n=q\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const std::uint64_t q = 1000003;
  std::uint64_t k = 1000000;
  while (!alg::is_probable_prime(k * q + 1)) ++k;
  const std::uint64_t p = k * q + 1, g = alg::primitive_root(p), gq = alg::mod_pow(g, (p - 1) / q, p);
  std::print("p = {} = {}·q + 1, primitive root g = {}, g_q = g^((p-1)/q) = {} has order {}.\n\n", p, k, g, gq,
             alg::element_order(gq, p));

  std::mt19937_64 rng(11);
  double full = 0, sub = 0;
  const int trials = 20;
  for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
    const std::uint64_t x = rng() % q, h = alg::mod_pow(gq, x, p);
    const auto a = alg::baby_step_giant_step_trace(gq, h, p);     // n = p - 1
    const auto b = alg::baby_step_giant_step_trace(gq, h, p, q);  // n = q
    if (a.exponent != x || b.exponent != x) std::print("mismatch\n");
    full += static_cast<double>(a.baby_steps + a.giant_steps);
    sub += static_cast<double>(b.baby_steps + b.giant_steps);
  }
  std::print("| search size \\(n\\) | table size \\(m\\) | mean steps |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| \\(p-1\\) | {} | {:.0f} |\n", alg::baby_step_giant_step_trace(gq, 1, p).table_size, full / trials);
  std::print("| \\(q\\) | {} | {:.0f} |\n", alg::baby_step_giant_step_trace(gq, 1, p, q).table_size, sub / trials);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
p = 1000047000133 = 1000044·q + 1, primitive root g = 2, g_q = g^((p-1)/q) = 866621728031 has order 1000003.

| search size \(n\) | table size \(m\) | mean steps |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| \(p-1\) | 1000024 | 1000025 |
| \(q\) | 1001 | 1493 |
// result_end

Searching the subgroup of order \(q\) took 1,493 steps on average, \(1.49\sqrt q\). Searching
as if \(g_q\) generated the whole group took about a million: the table of \(\sqrt{p-1}\approx10^6\)
entries is built in full even though \(g_q\) has only \(q\) distinct powers, so the work is the
table, and one giant step then always hits. Knowing the order of \(g\) saves a factor of 670
here. The prime \(p=1{,}000{,}047{,}000{,}133\) is weak in exactly this way:
\(p-1=1{,}000{,}044\cdot q\), and Pohlig–Hellman would solve logarithms to the base \(g\) one
subgroup at a time.

> Summary

- Writing \(x=im+j\) turns \(g^x\equiv h\) into the collision \(g^j\equiv h\,g^{-mi}\) between
  \(m\) stored baby steps and at most \(\lceil n/m\rceil\) giant steps.
- The worst-case work \(m+n/m\) is minimised at \(m=\sqrt n\) (\(2\sqrt n\)), the mean
  \(m+n/(2m)\) at \(m=\sqrt{n/2}\) (\(1.41\sqrt n\)); a sweep at \(p=1{,}000{,}003\) matches
  both.
- From \(10^3\) to \(10^{12}\) the work grows exactly as \(\sqrt p\); a 40-bit Diffie–Hellman
  secret falls in about 0.1 s, against about half an hour for trying exponents in order.
- Memory, \(\sqrt n\) table entries, is the real limit, and searching a prime-order subgroup
  costs \(\sqrt q\) rather than \(\sqrt p\), the basis of Pohlig–Hellman.
